# Boss worker rules

You are running inside a Boss-managed worker session. The engine
spawned you in a leased cube workspace and observes this session
via claude hooks.
For ordinary pre-push validation, run `checkleft run` with no flags; use
`checkleft --all` only in CI, when modifying checkleft itself, or with a
strong stated justification.

## Pull requests are the deliverable

**A task is not complete until a PR exists.** Local commits are NOT enough.

- Open a PR with `cube pr create` once commits exist and tests pass.
- **If a PR already exists** (resuming or addressing review),
push new commits to it with `cube pr update`; do NOT open a
duplicate. `cube pr create` is safe to retry: if a prior call
already created the PR (e.g. your tool killed an earlier
invocation on a timeout but the push had actually landed), it
returns that PR's URL instead of erroring. Use `cube pr update`
only when you have new commits to push onto an already-open PR;
it errors if none does. Check first with `boss pr status` — one
local round trip against the engine, not GitHub. Do NOT rely on
`boss context`'s `task.pr_url` field for this: it is NULL for a
revision task by design (a revision never owns its own PR — the
chain root does), so an empty `task.pr_url` does NOT mean "no
PR exists" when you're a revision worker. `boss pr status`
resolves your actually-bound PR correctly either way.
- Do not hard-wrap PR bodies.
- **NEVER pass the PR body as `--body "<inline text>"`** — the shell
evaluates backticks and `$(...)` inside double-quoted strings, which
corrupts any body that contains inline code. Always write the body to
a file and use `--body-file` (see the recipe below).
- Print the PR URL on its own line as the last thing in your final response.
- Before pushing, run `jj diff -r @`. If the diff is empty,
do NOT commit, push, or open a PR — stop and explain.

## Checking your own PR's state

Boss already stores most of what you'd otherwise ask GitHub for.
These read your own PR only — never another run's — and cost one
local round trip against the engine, not a GitHub API call:

- `boss pr status` — includes your resolved `pr_url`, the
cheapest way to answer "do I already have a PR?" before
deciding between `cube pr create` and `cube pr update`. Prefer
this over `boss context`'s `task.pr_url` field: that field is
NULL for a revision task by design (the chain root owns the PR,
not the revision), so it reads as "no PR" even when one exists.
`boss pr status` also returns `mergeable`, `merge_state_status`,
`head_sha`, and `observed_at` for your own PR, e.g.:
```sh
boss pr status --json
```
This is Boss's **last stored observation from the merge poller,
not live GitHub truth** — `observed_at` (Unix epoch seconds) is
the timestamp of that observation, not of your call. Right after
a push, the stored snapshot usually still reflects the *pre-push*
state. If you need current state (e.g. right after `cube pr
update` to see whether the push cleared a conflict), pass
`--refresh` for one bounded, rate-limited live check:
```sh
boss pr status --refresh --json
```
A refresh can be silently throttled (`refresh_throttled: true`
in the response) if the engine-wide budget is exhausted — in
that case you still get the last stored snapshot, never an error
or a hang. Never loop calling `--refresh` waiting for a state
change: the engine's own merge poller is what watches your PR to
green after you push, not you — the same "do not babysit CI"
principle your task prompt states applies here too.
- `boss pr body` — the PR title and body/description Boss
snapshotted when this run started, for a read-modify-write of
the description without a `gh pr view` round trip. If the
response's `body` is null (`--json`) or says "(none stored)"
(text), it means this run began a brand-new PR flow — there is
nothing to diff against yet, you are about to write the first
description via `cube pr
create --body-file`. It does NOT mean the PR has an empty
description; an intentionally empty one is stored as `""`, not
null. Only fall back to `gh pr view --json body` if you must
confirm the null case is not a fetch failure rather than a
new-PR flow.

If a `boss` command fails to run at all — not found, or it exits
non-zero without answering — that is a finding, not noise. Say so
explicitly in your final response and name the command. Do NOT
quietly drop the step and carry on: a create-vs-update decision
made without `boss pr status` is a decision made blind, and
nobody downstream can tell that from a clean transcript.

